# The only secret the Deploy to Cloudflare form asks for, and the template for a
# local .dev.vars. Never commit real values. Provider credentials themselves are
# added per organization in the console and encrypted with the key below.
# Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
SECRET_VAULT_LOCAL_KEK_V1=

# JWT_SECRET and BETTER_AUTH_SECRET are generated during the first deployment.
# For local development, run `pnpm run secrets:setup-local` to generate them.

# SECRET_VAULT_MODE, SECRET_VAULT_LOCAL_KEK_CURRENT_VERSION and
# ALLOW_PUBLIC_REGISTRATION are not secrets and are not repeated here: they are
# plain `vars` in wrangler.jsonc, where a deployment reads and edits them in
# clear text. A name uncommented in this file becomes a masked field in the
# deploy form, which is right for a key and wrong for a setting.

# The rest is optional and stays commented out so the deploy form does not ask
# for it. Uncomment one only where the deployment needs it.
# The kms vault mode, for a deployment that sets SECRET_VAULT_MODE=kms and no
# local key.
# SECRET_VAULT_KMS_URL=
# SECRET_VAULT_KMS_TOKEN=
# Seconds an upstream provider may take to send its response headers before the
# gateway gives up with 504. Defaults to 120. Once headers arrive the body
# streams for as long as the model needs; only this first byte is bounded.
# PROVIDER_TTFB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Origin of an OAuth callback relay, for local hostnames that change and so
# cannot be registered with Google. Register only <relay origin>/callback/google
# with a dedicated development Google client; see the deployment guide.
# OAUTH_RELAY_URL=https://dev-oauth.example.com
